{"type":"WebSearch","query":"vitepress markdown-it-anchor slugify duplicate heading custom id suffix hello-1","content":"**VitePress generates heading IDs via `@mdit/plugin-anchor` (a drop-in replacement for `markdown-it-anchor` with identical unique-slug handling).** Duplicate headings automatically get a numeric suffix (e.g., first "Hello" becomes `id="hello"`, the next becomes `id="hello-1"`). This uses `uniqueSlugStartIndex` (default `1`) and the internal uniqueness logic `${slug}-${i}`.[[1]](https://github.com/valeriangalliat/markdown-it-anchor)

You can customize this in `.vitepress/config.ts` (or `.js`) via the `markdown.anchor` option, which is passed through to the plugin. Common options include `slugify`, `uniqueSlugStartIndex`, `getTokensText`, `permalink`, `tabIndex`, and `failOnNonUnique`.

### Basic customization (slugify + duplicate suffix start index)
```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vitepress'

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    anchor: {
      // Custom slugify (default is a simple lowercase + hyphen version)
      slugify: (str) =>
        str
          .toLowerCase()
          .trim()
          .replace(/[^\w\s-]/g, '')
          .replace(/[\s_-]+/g, '-'),
      uniqueSlugStartIndex: 1, // Default: first duplicate is -1, then -2, etc.
      // Other options: level, permalink, getTokensText, tabIndex, failOnNonUnique, etc.
    }
  }
})
```

- Change `uniqueSlugStartIndex` to `2` (or another number) if you want the first duplicate to start at `-2` (some older plugins/GitHub-slugger variants used this).
- For explicit custom IDs in Markdown, use the suffix syntax: `# Heading {#my-custom-id}`. Duplicates of explicit IDs throw by default (`failOnNonUnique: true`); set it to `false` to suffix them instead. Auto-generated IDs always suffix on collision.[[2]](https://vitepress.dev/guide/markdown)

### Fully custom duplicate suffix (e.g., change `-1` format)
The uniqueness helper is internal (`uniq = \`${slug}-${i}\``), so override it with `slugifyWithState` (supported for accessing `state`/`state.env` per document). Track used slugs yourself:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vitepress'

const usedSlugs = new Set<string>() // Reset per page if needed; or attach to state.env

export default defineConfig({
  markdown: {
    anchor: {
      slugifyWithState: (title, state) => {
        // Your custom slugify
        let slug = title
          .toLowerCase()
          .trim()
          .replace(/[^\w\s-]/g, '')
          .replace(/[\s_-]+/g, '-')

        // Custom uniqueness (example: hello_1, hello_2 instead of hello-1)
        let i = 1
        let unique = slug
        while (usedSlugs.has(unique) || (state.env?.slugs && state.env.slugs[unique])) {
          unique = `${slug}_${i}` // Your custom suffix format
          i++
        }
        usedSlugs.add(unique)
        // Optionally store on state.env for other plugins
        if (!state.env.slugs) state.env.slugs = {}
        state.env.slugs[unique] = true
        return unique
      }
    }
  }
})
```

Reset the tracker (e.g., via a VitePress hook or per-render) if you reuse state across pages. This also works with VitePress's built-in `getTokensText` override for handling images/emojis in headings.

See the VitePress Markdown docs for the full `markdown.anchor` example and the plugin docs (linked from there) for all options. Test by adding duplicate headings and inspecting the generated `<h*>` `id` attributes or permalinks. If you're on an older VitePress version still using `markdown-it-anchor` directly, the options are the same.","citations":["https://github.com/valeriangalliat/markdown-it-anchor","https://vitepress.dev/guide/markdown"],"allowed_domains":["github.com","vitepress.dev"]}

